{"authored" [{"question" "q-123224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037744/state.json" "file_sha256" "7607ec4719b83b778accc6f508bf9e6d3c093f93f7686b6d22ff4c580be1dacb" "basis" 1276 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-123224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037744/state.json" "file_sha256" "7607ec4719b83b778accc6f508bf9e6d3c093f93f7686b6d22ff4c580be1dacb" "basis" 1276 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-123224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "